me><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>KATO HIROMASA</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>MITSUBISHI CHEM CORP</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>NAKATANI MASAFUMI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>DIA INSTRUMENTS CO., LTD.</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KATO, HIROMASA</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>MITSUBISHI CHEMICAL CORPORATION</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>NAKATANI, MASAFUMI</name></addressbook></applicant></applicants><inventors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>KATO HIROMASA</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>NAKATANI MASAFUMI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KATO, HIROMASA</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>NAKATANI, MASAFUMI</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A method of detecting constant-current polarization voltage, in which polarization voltage is sequentially detected for each current pulse applied to a detection electrode during an electrochemical analysis. In this method, polarization voltage is detected a time (t0) after each current pulse applied. This method can be applied to an apparatus for the Karl-Fischer technique.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention concerne un procédé de détection d'une tension de polarisation à courant constant, le procédé consistant à détecter séquentiellement une tension de polarisation pour chaque impulsion de courant appliquée à une électrode de détection lors d'une analyse électrochimique. Dans ce procédé, la tension de polarisation est détectée à un moment (t0) après chaque impulsion de courant appliquée. On peut utiliser ce procédé avec un appareil pour appliquer la technique de Karl Fischer.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>